## Problem

In `command-palette.svelte.ts:50`, the `back()` method calls `this.close()`. In a plain object literal, `this` is only correct when called as a method on the object. If `back` is destructured or passed as a callback, `this` is `undefined` (strict mode).

Even in the working case, using `this` in a plain object factory is fragile and inconsistent with the rest of the codebase which avoids `this` entirely.

## Suggested fix

Call the close logic directly instead of via `this`:

```ts
back() {
  if (stack.length > 1) {
    stack = stack.slice(0, -1);
    selectedIndex = 0;
  } else {
    open = false;
    query = "";
    selectedIndex = 0;
    stack = [];
  }
}
```

Or extract a `reset()` inner function and call it from both `close()` and `back()`.

## Problem

`renderMarkdown()` in `markdown.ts:79` calls `marked.use()` on every invocation, replacing the global renderer each time. This:

1. **Accumulates renderer entries** — `marked.use()` pushes onto an internal renderer stack, growing unboundedly over the app's lifetime
2. **Races with concurrent renders** — if two renders are in-flight, they share the same `headings` array via closure. After the highlighter is cached, concurrent calls race on `marked.use()`
3. **Wasteful** — the renderer configuration is identical each time except for the `headings` array capture

## Suggested fix

Create a new `Marked` instance per call instead of mutating the global singleton:

```ts
export async function renderMarkdown(source: string) {
  const generation = ++renderGeneration;
  const hl = await getHighlighter();
  const headings: TocHeading[] = [];
  const instance = new Marked({
    renderer: {
      code({ text, lang }) { /* ... */ },
      heading({ text, depth }) { headings.push({ id, text, level: depth }); /* ... */ }
    }
  });
  const html = await instance.parse(source);
  return { html, generation, headings };
}
```

Alternatively, extract headings in a post-processing pass (DOM walk) rather than via renderer hooks.

## Problem

`EmptyState.svelte:14` loads a Google Font via CDN:

```html
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Comfortaa:wght@700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />
```

This is a Tauri desktop app. Issues:
1. Empty state looks broken when offline (font fails to load)
2. Privacy/tracking concern — Google sees every app launch
3. Flash of unstyled text before the font loads
4. Loading fonts in a child component via \`<svelte:head>\` is an anti-pattern

## Suggested fix

1. Download the Comfortaa 700 weight font file
2. Place it in \`static/fonts/Comfortaa-Bold.woff2\`
3. Add an \`@font-face\` declaration in \`app.css\` or \`base.css\`:

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: 'Comfortaa';
  src: url('/fonts/Comfortaa-Bold.woff2') format('woff2');
  font-weight: 700;
  font-display: swap;
}
```

4. Remove the \`<link>\` tag from EmptyState.svelte

## Problem

`TabBar.svelte` and `FloatingDock.svelte` hardcode hex colors from GitHub Dark theme:
- TabBar: \`#161b22\`, \`#30363d\`, \`#21262d\`, \`#8b949e\`, \`#c9d1d9\`, \`#0d1117\`
- FloatingDock: \`#21262d\`, \`#30363d\`, \`#8b949e\`, \`#c9d1d9\`, \`#f0f6fc\`, \`#58a6ff\`

These components are always visible regardless of the active theme. With any light theme or non-GitHub theme, the chrome clashes badly with the content area.

## Suggested fix

Define CSS custom properties for chrome/UI elements in the theme system:

```css
/* base.css or a new chrome-vars layer */
:root {
  --chrome-bg: #161b22;
  --chrome-border: #30363d;
  --chrome-text: #c9d1d9;
  --chrome-text-muted: #8b949e;
  --chrome-accent: #58a6ff;
}
```

Each theme overrides these variables. TabBar and FloatingDock consume them:

```css
.tab-bar { background: var(--chrome-bg); border-color: var(--chrome-border); }
```

This makes the entire app respond to theme changes, not just the markdown content area.

## Problem

In `preferences.svelte.ts:241`:
```ts
select: (v) => setContentWidthValue(v as ContentWidth),
```

The `ChoiceSetting.select` callback accepts `string`, but this cast silently accepts any string. Same pattern appears wherever choice settings wire up their select handlers.

In `commands.ts:91`:
```ts
const zoomSetting = prefs.settings.find((s) => s.id === "zoom") as RangeSetting | undefined;
```

Uses `as` to bypass the discriminated union when a type guard would be correct.

## Suggested fix

Make `ChoiceSetting` generic over its value type:

```ts
interface ChoiceSetting<V extends string = string> {
  type: "choice";
  id: string;
  label: string;
  options: { value: V; label: string; swatch?: string }[];
  value: V;
  select: (value: V) => void | Promise<void>;
}
```

Then the content-width setting receives `ContentWidth` directly, no cast needed. Theme setting receives `ThemeId`, etc.

For the `commands.ts` cast, use a type guard:

```ts
function isRangeSetting(s: SettingDef): s is RangeSetting {
  return s.type === 'range';
}
const zoomSetting = prefs.settings.find(s => s.id === "zoom" && isRangeSetting(s));
```

## Problem

In `markdown.ts:64`, `renderGeneration` is a single global counter. When multiple tabs render concurrently:

1. File A starts rendering (generation 5)
2. File B starts rendering (generation 6)
3. File A completes — `isLatestRender(5)` returns false, discarding a valid render

In `files.svelte.ts:55`, `isLatestRender(generation)` checks against this global counter, meaning background tab renders can be silently discarded.

## Suggested fix

Track generation per file path:

```ts
const renderGenerations = new Map<string, number>();

export function nextGeneration(path: string): number {
  const gen = (renderGenerations.get(path) ?? 0) + 1;
  renderGenerations.set(path, gen);
  return gen;
}

export function isLatestRender(path: string, generation: number): boolean {
  return renderGenerations.get(path) === generation;
}
```

This allows concurrent renders for different files to complete independently.

## Problem

In `+page.svelte:66`:

```ts
onMount(() => {
  prefs.init();
```

`prefs.init()` is `async` and returns a `Promise<void>`. The floating promise means theme loading errors are silently swallowed. If `themeState.setTheme()` fails during init, the user sees no feedback and the app may be in a broken state.

## Suggested fix

Either catch errors explicitly:

```ts
onMount(() => {
  prefs.init().catch(console.error);
```

Or make the onMount callback async (Svelte supports this, the return value is just ignored):

```ts
onMount(async () => {
  await prefs.init();
```

## Problem

In `files.svelte.ts:23-31`:

```ts
tabs.add({ ...result, rendered: html, headings });
await invoke("watch_file", { path: result.path });
```

1. If the file changes between `read_file` and `watch_file`, that change is lost — the tab shows stale content with no watcher to catch the update
2. If `watch_file` throws, the tab is already added with no watcher — it will never receive live updates

## Suggested fix

Establish the watch before or atomically with the read. Sequence should be:

```ts
await invoke("watch_file", { path: result.path });
const result = await invoke("read_file", { path });
// Now render and add tab
tabs.add({ ...result, rendered: html, headings });
```

Or handle the error case:

```ts
try {
  tabs.add({ ...result, rendered: html, headings });
  await invoke("watch_file", { path: result.path });
} catch (e) {
  // Remove tab or show error indicator
  tabs.close(tabs.items.findIndex(t => t.path === result.path));
}
```

## Problem

Multiple fire-and-forget promises and missing error handling:

1. **\`files.svelte.ts:64-75\`** — \`openFileDialog()\` can throw from \`openDialog()\` but has no try/catch. The caller in \`+page.svelte:45\` calls it without \`await\` or \`.catch()\`.

2. **\`Preferences.svelte:20-21\`** — \`checkDefaultViewer()\` calls \`invoke<boolean>("is_default_markdown_viewer")\` without try/catch. If the Rust command fails, the component breaks silently.

3. **\`+page.svelte:96-99\`** — \`listen\` return values could reject if \`listen\` failed. Unlisten promises in cleanup lack \`.catch()\`.

## Suggested fix

Add try/catch at system boundaries (Tauri IPC is an external system boundary):

```ts
export async function openFileDialog() {
  try {
    const result = await openDialog({ ... });
    if (result) {
      const paths = Array.isArray(result) ? result : [result];
      await Promise.all(paths.map(openFile));
    }
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('Failed to open file dialog:', e);
  }
}
```

## Problem

In \`toc.svelte.ts:17-19\`:

```ts
setHeadings(h: TocHeading[]) {
  headings = h;
  activeId = h[0]?.id ?? null;
}
```

When a file is live-reloaded, \`tabs.update\` updates the tab's headings, which triggers the \`\$effect\` in \`+page.svelte:104-107\`, calling \`setHeadings\`, which unconditionally resets \`activeId\` to the first heading. This loses the user's scroll position during live editing.

## Suggested fix

Only reset \`activeId\` when heading IDs actually change:

```ts
setHeadings(h: TocHeading[]) {
  const oldIds = headings.map(h => h.id);
  const newIds = h.map(h => h.id);
  headings = h;
  // Only reset if the heading structure changed, not just content
  if (JSON.stringify(oldIds) !== JSON.stringify(newIds)) {
    activeId = h[0]?.id ?? null;
  }
}
```

Or better yet, if the current \`activeId\` still exists in the new headings, keep it.

## Problem

In \`scroll-spy.ts:23\`:

```ts
if (h.offsetTop - node.offsetTop <= scrollTop + SCROLL_OFFSET)
```

\`offsetTop\` is relative to \`offsetParent\`, which may not be \`node\` if there are intermediate positioned elements. The \`zoom\` CSS property on \`.content\`, theme CSS, or the article element could introduce a positioned ancestor, causing \`offsetTop\` to be relative to the wrong element.

## Suggested fix

Use \`getBoundingClientRect()\` which is always relative to the viewport and unaffected by intermediate positioning:

```ts
const nodeRect = node.getBoundingClientRect();
for (const h of headings) {
  const headingRect = h.getBoundingClientRect();
  const relativeTop = headingRect.top - nodeRect.top + node.scrollTop;
  if (relativeTop <= node.scrollTop + SCROLL_OFFSET) {
    currentId = h.id;
  }
}
```

Or walk the \`offsetParent\` chain to accumulate the true offset.

## Problem

In `Preferences.svelte:430-453`, the slider has `outline: none` without a `:focus-visible` replacement. When a user tabs to the slider, there is no visible focus indicator, breaking keyboard navigation accessibility.

## Suggested fix

```css
.slider:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--chrome-accent, #58a6ff);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
```

## Problem

`getTabs()`, `getPreferences()`, `getCommandPalette()`, `getToc()`, and `getThemeState()` all return anonymous object types. Consumers needing the type must use `ReturnType<typeof getTabs>` (seen in `commands.ts:24`).

This makes API contracts invisible, breaks IDE jump-to-definition, and means accidental changes to the return shape are silent.

## Suggested fix

Export named interfaces from each module:

```ts
// tabs.svelte.ts
export interface TabsAPI {
  readonly items: Tab[];
  readonly active: Tab | undefined;
  readonly activeIndex: number;
  add(tab: Tab): void;
  close(index: number): void;
  update(path: string, data: Partial<Tab>): void;
}

export const tabs: TabsAPI = { /* ... */ };
```

Apply consistently across all five state modules. This makes the public API explicit and documented.

## Problem

Several switch/if-chains on discriminated unions lack exhaustive checks:

1. **`commands.ts:39-72`** — `commandFromSetting(setting: SettingDef)` uses if/return for 'choice' and falls through to range. Adding a third setting type would silently use range logic.

2. **`+page.svelte:76-87`** — zoom event payload is `string` with no `default` case. Unexpected payloads silently do nothing.

## Suggested fix

Use `satisfies never` for exhaustive checking:

```ts
// commands.ts
switch (setting.type) {
  case 'choice': return { /* ... */ };
  case 'range': return { /* ... */ };
  default: return setting.type satisfies never;
}

// +page.svelte zoom handler
type ZoomAction = 'in' | 'out' | 'reset';
switch (event.payload as ZoomAction) {
  case 'in': /* ... */ break;
  case 'out': /* ... */ break;
  case 'reset': /* ... */ break;
  default: (event.payload as never) satisfies never;
}
```

This turns missed cases into compile-time errors when new variants are added.

## Problem

In `preferences.svelte.ts:70`:

```ts
return raw ? JSON.parse(raw) : {};
```

`JSON.parse` returns `any`, which leaks into `StoredPreferences`. While the individual field checks on lines 334-338 provide some runtime safety, the initial parse is unvalidated — corrupted localStorage data could have unexpected shapes.

## Suggested fix

Add a parse-and-validate function:

```ts
function loadStored(): StoredPreferences {
  try {
    const raw = localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
    if (!raw) return {};
    const parsed: unknown = JSON.parse(raw);
    if (typeof parsed !== 'object' || parsed === null) return {};
    return parsed as StoredPreferences;
  } catch {
    return {};
  }
}
```

This catches corrupted JSON and ensures the parsed value is at least an object before treating it as `StoredPreferences`.

## Problem

In `files.svelte.ts:70-73`:

```ts
for (const path of paths) {
  await openFile(path);
}
```

When opening multiple files (e.g., selecting 10 files in the open dialog), each file waits for the previous to fully complete (read + render + watch). File reads and watch registrations are independent operations.

## Suggested fix

```ts
await Promise.all(paths.map(openFile));
```

Since each `openFile` call reads a different file, renders independently, and registers its own watcher, these are safe to parallelize. The only ordering concern is tab insertion order — if that matters, use `Promise.allSettled` and add tabs in the original order after all complete.

## Problem

In `preferences.svelte.ts:209`, `get settings()` builds a new array of 6 `SettingDef` objects every time it's read. This getter is accessed in `$derived` computations and rendered in `Preferences.svelte`. Since it creates new objects each time, Svelte's reactivity sees these as changed on every read, potentially causing unnecessary re-renders of the entire settings UI.

## Suggested fix

Make it a `$derived` at module level so Svelte can track the actual reactive dependencies and only recompute when the underlying values change:

```ts
const _settings = $derived<SettingDef[]>([
  { type: 'choice', id: 'theme', /* ... */ },
  { type: 'range', id: 'zoom', /* ... */ },
  // ...
]);

// In the exported object:
get settings() { return _settings; }
```

## Problem

\`TabBar.svelte\`, \`FloatingDock.svelte\`, and \`EmptyState.svelte\` use inline types for \`\$props()\`:

```ts
let { onclose }: { onclose: (index: number) => void } = \$props();
```

While \`TableOfContents.svelte\` correctly uses a named interface:

```ts
interface Props { /* ... */ }
let { ... }: Props = \$props();
```

## Suggested fix

Use named \`Props\` interfaces consistently across all components:

```ts
interface Props {
  onclose: (index: number) => void;
}
let { onclose }: Props = \$props();
```

This is the Svelte 5 convention. Named interfaces are reusable, self-documenting, and visible in IDE tooltips.

## Problem

\`scrollSpy\` in \`scroll-spy.ts\` and \`copyCode\` in \`copy-code.ts\` match the Svelte action contract \`(node, params) => { destroy }\` but have inferred return types. If either accidentally deviates from the action contract, there's no compile-time error.

## Suggested fix

```ts
import type { Action } from 'svelte/action';

export const scrollSpy: Action<HTMLElement, (id: string | null) => void> = (node, onActiveChange) => {
  // ...
};

export const copyCode: Action<HTMLElement> = (node) => {
  // ...
};
```

This ensures the functions conform to Svelte's action interface at compile time.

## Problem

In `themes/registry.ts`, `ThemeMeta.id` is typed as `string`. The 22 theme IDs are a fixed set of string literals. Typos in theme IDs are not caught at compile time.

## Suggested fix

```ts
export const themes = [
  { id: 'github-dark', name: 'GitHub Dark', /* ... */ },
  // ...
] as const satisfies readonly ThemeMeta[];

export type ThemeId = typeof themes[number]['id'];
// ThemeId = 'github-dark' | 'dracula' | 'nord' | ...
```

Then use `ThemeId` wherever theme IDs are referenced (localStorage keys, `setTheme()` parameter, etc.) for compile-time safety.

Same pattern applies to `settingsSections` in `preferences.svelte.ts:46` and `TAB_COLORS` in `tabs.svelte.ts:12`.
